# Reconstruction Theory Bridge

## Purpose

This document investigates the relationship between the asymptotic closure framework developed in the refinement-stability program and existing functional-analytic reconstruction theories.

The objective is NOT proving the Yang–Mills Mass Gap problem.

Instead, the purpose is identifying whether the asymptotic closure mechanism corresponds to, extends, or partially overlaps known operator-theoretic reconstruction frameworks.

This document represents the first explicit reconstruction-theory-bridge phase of the refinement-stability program.

---

# 1. Motivation

Previous development progressively reduced the refinement-stability framework toward a theorem-level closure structure:

\[
R_N(z)
\to
R_\infty(z),
\]

combined with preservation of the resolvent identity:

\[
R_\infty(z)-R_\infty(w)
=
(w-z)R_\infty(z)R_\infty(w).
\]

This produced the first candidate closure theorem:

\[
R_\infty(z)
=
(z-T_\infty)^{-1}.
\]

The framework now reaches a crucial mathematical transition:

```text
Does the asymptotic closure mechanism
already exist
within known functional analysis?
```

The present document investigates this question directly.

---

# 2. Central Bridge Principle

The framework now adopts the following principle:

```text
a mathematically meaningful asymptotic closure program
must connect
to existing operator-reconstruction theory.
```

This principle becomes central for future rigorization attempts.

